00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:14.619
Twenty years ago, the western world didn’t
really think of China as a market.

00:00:14.619 --> 00:00:19.529
The country was an asset for the west’s
own market, sure, but only for the reason

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:23.929
that it was a large, poor country that could
act as a source of low-cost labor.

00:00:23.929 --> 00:00:29.460
Today, though, with an economy ten times larger
that that of just twenty years ago, the western

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.350
world, or at least the properly informed western
world, no longer thinks of Chi na as that

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:41.110
poor, low-cost labor asset, but rather as
a significant, attractive market itself.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:46.030
Whereas in the past China made for the west,
today China is the very market that the western

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.750
world focuses their attention on.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:53.469
With a rapidly growing middle and upper class,
businesses around the world have flooded in

00:00:53.469 --> 00:00:57.590
to cater to China’s newly wealthy in the
same way that they have long served the rich

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.680
in the wealthy western world.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:04.269
Industries ranging from hospitality, to tourism,
to e-commerce, to beauty, and more are all

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:09.659
thriving there with both home-grown and foreign-born
players establishing their spots in the Chinese

00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:13.830
market, but one enormous industry that is
still emerging and evolving in the Middle

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:17.049
Kingdom is that of sports.

00:01:17.049 --> 00:01:22.650
Sports is a monstrous global industry, but
nowhere is that industry more enormous than

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.200
in the US.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.560
The NFL, MLB, NBA, and NHL represent the first,
second, fourth, and six most valuable sports

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:36.189
leagues in the world and collectively bring
in more than $30 billion in annual revenue.

00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:40.409
However, American sports are not purely an
American industry.

00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:44.270
Between merchandizing, league partnerships,
TV and streaming rights, and other revenue

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:49.369
streams, American sports leagues actually
earn a considerable amount of money outside

00:01:49.369 --> 00:01:50.700
of America.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.590
China is a market worth targeting for any
business, but it has a particular appeal for

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:57.229
sports leagues.

00:01:57.229 --> 00:02:02.310
Beyond its status as a large and relatively
recently wealthy nation, China has a government

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:06.729
willing and interested in investing in foreign
sports—the kind of foreign sports that would

00:02:06.729 --> 00:02:09.790
earn them prestige in international competitions.

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:14.090
Each of the four major American sports leagues
are the dominant leagues in the world for

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:20.250
their respective sports, so any interest in
each sport will lead to interest in the league.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:25.560
Consequentially, each league has its own strategy
to grow Chinese interest both in their sport

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.050
and their league.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:32.890
Baseball is incredibly popular in nearby Taiwan,
South Korea, and Japan, and so there was reason

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:36.560
to believe that the sport could too become
popular in China.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.750
Despite this, though, Major League Baseball
has demonstrated the least interest in the

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:45.060
market and the market has demonstrated the
least interest in the MLB.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:49.870
The league’s most tangible effort to attract
Chinese audiences took place back in 2008

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:54.040
when two pre-season games between the Padres
and Dodgers were played in Beijing.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:58.070
Since then, the history of baseball in China
has been fraught with difficulties with their

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:02.730
national league, which operated in cooperation
with the American MLB, suspending operations

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:11.120
in 2012, restarting in 2014, suspending operations
again in 2016, then starting up again in 2019

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.010
with just four teams.

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:17.970
The MLB has also long-operated three, “development
centers,” where top Chinese baseball players

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:22.920
are trained, although, none of the players
from these centers have ever actually seen

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.590
action in the MLB itself.

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:29.940
One can find press release upon press release
about different renewed efforts by the MLB

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:36.010
to grow the sport in China, however, all signs
indicate that these have not resulted in much.

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:40.310
Evidence of this can be seen on the league’s
official Weibo account—one of the top social

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:44.970
networks in China—which has only 650,000
followers.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:50.330
The story is not markedly different with the
NHL, but the history of its efforts is shorter.

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:55.840
It put on exhibition matches between the Kings
and Canucks in Shanghai and Beijing in 2017,

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:59.990
then between the Bruins and Flames in Beijing
and Shenzhen in 2018.

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:03.980
These games were generally well received and
were attended by healthy-sized audiences,

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:08.460
but there was a conspicuous lack of follow-up,
with no 2019 games in China, reportedly due

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:10.050
to issues with booking arenas.

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:14.710
Since then, though, they’ve doubled-down,
sending Stanley Cup Champion Alex Ovechkin

00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:19.329
to the country in summer 2019 to promote the
sport, opening an office in Beijing, and hiring

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:20.920
a China marketing head.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:25.680
Unfortunately for them, though, the little
attention China pays to Hockey is not primarily

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.840
directed to the NHL.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.810
The second most lucrative Hockey league in
the world, the Kontinental Hockey League,

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:36.970
added a Chinese team in 2016—the HC Kunlun
Red Star.

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:41.780
This team is no phenomenon yet, with the lowest
average game attendance in the league, but

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:46.820
it does mean that a Chinese hockey fan would
much more likely pay attention to the KHL

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:47.870
over the NHL.

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:53.600
Still, though, the NHL seems to get somewhat
more respectable Chinese engagement—demonstrated

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.710
by their just over 1 million fans on Weibo.

00:04:56.710 --> 00:05:00.100
American football, though, is where it starts
to get interesting.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:06.350
The NFL has generated a real fanbase among
Chinese—not a large one, but it certainly

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:07.780
exists.

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:12.030
This is despite the fact that they are the
only of the four leagues that has not played

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:13.680
a game in China.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:17.670
There is talk of putting one on in the country
at the start of the 2020 season, although,

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:20.590
there has been talk before without result.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:26.030
The real potential for the NFL, though, comes
not through in-country games, but rather,

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:27.800
through digital distribution.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:32.340
Each week, Tencent’s streaming platforms
broadcast a number of games, which each have

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:36.050
an average viewership of more than two million
people.

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:40.520
That’s despite the awkward broadcast times
in the mid-morning, given the time difference.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:45.130
How much the NFL was paid for these broadcast
rights is not publicly known, but with those

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:47.780
kind of numbers, it’s certainly not nothing.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:52.240
Beyond the league as a whole’s efforts,
the New England patriots have put a particular

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:54.860
level of effort into attracting Chinese fans.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:06:00.150
A huge benefit for them is that their star,
Tom Brady, has been learning and speaks a

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:02.200
rudimentary level of Mandarin.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:06.020
He hosted a Chinese-language show for the
team’s digital channels which attracted

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:09.740
sizable attention—over 15 million total
views.

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:14.200
Meanwhile, playing the sport itself is increasingly
popular in China.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:18.160
Having one’s children learn the American
sport has emerged as a status symbol, and

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:22.920
120,000 young Chinese have gone to private
training camps for American football.

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:28.870
Still, though, the NFL has a long way to go
to make China a sizable portion of its business.

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:34.800
On Weibo, for example, it has only attracted
slightly more fans than the NHL, at 1.3 million.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:39.920
The final of these four leagues, though, has
turned China into a sizable and now crucial

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:41.740
bit of its business.

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:46.730
Basketball has been played casually in China
since the late 1800s when American missionaries

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:48.400
first introduced the sport.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:53.590
As China liberalized in the latter half of
the 20th century, the NBA capitalized on this

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:58.360
and then-commissioner David Stern led an effort
to get their games televised in China.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:03.570
This attracted a moderate fanbase, but the
true, pivotal moment happened in 2002.

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:09.230
In that year, a young player from the Shanghai
Sharks entered the NBA draft and was picked

00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:11.620
first overall—Yao Ming.

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:17.091
He quickly became a sensation and reached
the NBA All-Star team eight times, but as

00:07:17.091 --> 00:07:23.010
much as he was a sensation in the US, he was
just as much or more back home in China.

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:28.140
The number of Chinese NBA fans grew exponentially
with every game he played, but eventually,

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:32.250
after a series of injury-plagued seasons,
Ming retired in 2011.

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:37.110
At the time, anyone would have assumed that
this would have been the end of the NBA’s

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:40.830
rise in China, but as it turned out, this
was not the case.

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:45.310
While at first Chinese fans may have tuned
in to watch Yao Ming, eventually they just

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:47.150
tuned in to watch the NBA.

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:52.580
In the decade following Ming’s retirement,
China grew into an enormous, crucial part

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:58.240
of the NBA’s business, representing more
than $500 million a year in revenue.

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:05.210
However, in October 2019, this was all threatened
by one tweet containing one image.

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:08.930
It read, “Fight for Freedom, Stand with
Hong Kong.”

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:13.770
This was, of course, a reference in support
of the widespread protests in Hong Kong, protests

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:18.449
commonly characterized as anti-Beijing, but
what’s more important is who this tweet

00:08:18.449 --> 00:08:23.860
was by—Daryl Morey, the general manager
of the Houston Rockets.

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:28.770
All of a sudden, deals fell apart, relationships
were severed, game broadcasts were cancelled—the

00:08:28.770 --> 00:08:33.490
NBA’s China market was under threat due
to a perception that the league was anti-China.

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:38.560
Unsurprisingly, for business reasons, the
NBA quickly issued what was essentially an

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:40.290
apology for this tweet.

00:08:40.290 --> 00:08:46.040
Of course, following that, the NBA both faced
criticism from China, for the original tweet,

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:49.230
and from the US, for apologizing for that
tweet.

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:53.160
Eventually, the scandal largely blew over
and relationships started to inch back to

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:58.690
normal, but this was all indicative of a bigger
issue with American sports’ expansion into

00:08:58.690 --> 00:08:59.690
China.

00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:05.010
What these leagues are doing is selling one
product to two very different markets.

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:10.160
Worldwide, the entertainment industry, of
which sports is a part of, has become increasingly

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:15.260
homogenized with more and more people watching
the same TV shows, listening to the same music,

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:19.480
and going to the same movies no matter which
country or culture they come from.

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:22.140
China, meanwhile, is different.

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:26.650
Chinese people largely watch different TV
shows, listen to different music, and go to

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:28.440
different movies than the rest of us.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:32.360
A lot of this is because of government censorship
and import policy.

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:37.490
For example, there’s a quota allowing only
a small number of foreign films into Chinese

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:39.010
cinemas each year.

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:42.570
Sports, though, can’t be treated like the
rest of entertainment.

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:47.850
The sports entertainment experience in the
21st century stretches far beyond just the

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:49.700
viewing of actual games.

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:55.430
In fact, watching the games in their entirety
is hardly even a requirement anymore.

00:09:55.430 --> 00:10:01.510
Sports is an all-encompassing, unscripted,
uncensored entertainment experience, and this

00:10:01.510 --> 00:10:02.670
is the problem for China.

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:07.670
It can’t just edit out an unflattering aspect
of sports in the same way it can with foreign

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:09.210
films.

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:12.560
Sports and politics in the US are intrinsically
intertwined.

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:17.430
Many athletes naturally take their place as
political activists given their influence,

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:20.700
but this is very different from in China.

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:26.320
In China, sports is a game where athletes
go and play, someone wins, and then everyone

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:27.320
goes home.

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:33.010
That’s because, in China, entertainment
and politics stay far, far away from each

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:38.650
other, or perhaps more accurately, entertainment
and opposition politics stay far, far away

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:39.740
from each other.

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:44.270
American sports will continue their march
towards increased relevancy in China as it,

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:47.970
as a country, has an undersaturated spectator
sports market.

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:52.730
Meanwhile, though, if these leagues become
a true phenomenon in China, they’ll have

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:57.660
to make more tough calls as to whether they
want to be a sport league for the US, or for

00:10:57.660 --> 00:11:00.380
the world.

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:04.180
In my opinion, the behind-the-scenes of sports
is fascinating.

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:08.770
If you agree, but feel you’ve gotten enough
of the business side for now, you can learn

00:11:08.770 --> 00:11:11.330
a little more about the physics side.

00:11:11.330 --> 00:11:15.190
You can learn how climbers figure our how
elastic their rope should be—elastic enough

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:18.750
to not give them whiplash when they fall,
but not elastic enough to let them hit the

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:22.720
ground; you can learn how energy transforms
when a skier descends a slope; you can learn

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:27.810
how archers account for gravity in their aim;
and you can learn all of this on Brilliant.

00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:32.180
These are just some of the interesting, engaging
things that you learn about while you’re

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:38.610
actually learning classical mechanics on Brilliant—something
decisively less engaging than climbing, skiing,

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:39.610
or archery.

